who drives that amazing car out there?

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:50.780
- He's over there.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:56.400
- It's with the cake.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:01.380
- All right, good morning.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:04.880
I am Al Simmons, Spawn, or, well, Al, well, I am both.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:05.720
- Hey.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:06.840
- I am both.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:07.680
Here, let me take you back

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.640
and show you what it's all about, Vanessa.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:11.440
- Al's roommate from college,

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:13.490
creator of the superhero Spawn,

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.350
named the Spawn character after Al.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.050
The same way that Clark Kent

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:20.230
is the alter ego of Superman,

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.330
Al Simmons is the alter ego of Spawn.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:25.800
- Todd McFarlane, who created Spawn, Vanessa,

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.020
he'll call up one of his artists

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:30.000
and they'll sit down and plot this out.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.120
to illustrate the story.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:35.320
It's the artist's job to take these thumbnails or rough

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:38.420
drafts and transform them into the Spawn characters we see

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:40.120
in the comic books every month.

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:42.770
These drawings are called pencils.

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.320
Once the pencils are done, the inker takes the pencil

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.240
sketches and goes over them with indie ink to bring out

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:49.240
the characters.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:50.840
How would you like to give inking a try?

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:52.830
I'd love to.

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:53.940
This is so cool.

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:57.520
I'm actually inking a page of Spawn for the next issue.

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:00.000
Inking is what creates the feeling of a comic.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.000
Like if there's a scary scene, you add more shadow.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.000
Or if you want to make the mood different,

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.000
you just lighten things up a bit.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.170
Come with me.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.350
We get to meet the creator of Spawn,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:12.210
Todd McFarlane.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:13.320
Todd?

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.190
He came back in there.

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.190
Yes.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:17.190
Hi, I'm Vanessa.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:18.190
Hey, Vanessa.

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:19.080
How are you doing today?

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.010
You know why I gave him such a big cape there?

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.010
The toughest thing to learn is to draw anatomy,

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.000
the human body.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.000
So if I cover it up with a drapery,

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.010
they go, "Wow, Todd, he knows how to draw the human body."

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.000
But the answer isn't it.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.920
I never actually see it, so I just trick people all the time, right?

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:37.920
So you're actually lazy to make a body, so you just put over a cape?

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:42.240
That's right. But although I'm lazy, they call it style.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:44.240
Drawings are scanned into a computer.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:48.950
And then, it's the colorist's job to bring every scene to life with color.

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:49.950
Take the pen. Cool.

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:52.950
And draw just like an outline, like you're drawing with a pencil.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:53.900
What a neat pen.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:57.080
Hit the J key, and that's the airbrush.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:59.080
And then you can just spray right over the top of your selection there.

00:19:59.080 --> 00:19:59.860
Cool.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.090
- See, it's getting lighter.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.840
- Now you can see what you've done.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.850
- Uh, I don't know, it's kind of funny.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.010
- And now, the lettering.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:13.630
This is how they put in all that neat dialogue.

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.070
They used to do it by hand,

00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:17.110
but now it's mostly done on computers.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.410
- What we have to do here, Vanessa,

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:21.500
is we have to take the black and white from the letterer

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:24.950
and color it, and basically cut and paste things

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:26.750
like crooom and dialogue,

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.590
and drop it on the actual artwork.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:29.860
So do you want to give it a try?

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.430
Yes.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:32.290
B.

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:35.930
And then go ahead and click on that, and then paste it down on there.

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:36.930
Oh!

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:37.930
Cool.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:38.970
Look at that.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:40.690
That's huge.

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:41.690
Vanessa, here it is.

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:42.970
The issue of Spawn we worked on together.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:43.970
Here, I autographed it for you.

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:44.970
Thanks so much.

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:50.410
You can get this issue of Spawn next month in comic book stores, but I think I'll go

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:52.010
read mine right now.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:59.220
Still coming up with new ideas yet to be conceived by science?

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.860
- Right past that, my PCR idea I'm changing

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.480
into a series of comic books.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:07.700
- Comic books?

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:10.600
- PCR man, the only one on the planet

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:13.300
who can program a VCR from a distance.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:15.880
He helps good people by programming their VCRs

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:17.160
with the shows they've missed,

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.240
and he taunts the bad guys by programming their VCRs

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:22.860
with the schlockiest TV shows on the planet,

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:25.600
24 hours a day.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.940
- So now that's the Tyler I know and love.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:34.590
See you next time.
